from pathlib import Path
from docx import Document
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
f=P.parent/'redshift_paper/temporal_redshift_paper.docx'
d=Document(f)
assert not any(p.text.startswith('Appendix G.') for p in d.paragraphs)
def p(t,s='Normal'):d.add_paragraph(t,s)
def h(t):p(t,'Heading 2')
def e(t):p(t,'Equation')
d.add_page_break()
p('Appendix G. Interaction constraints and five retained candidates','Heading 1')
p('This extension consolidates the calculations following Appendix F. The alternate universe remains physically nonexpanding and unscreened, with no Big Bang assumed. New interactions are allowed as hypotheses, but agreement with one observable is distinguished from a complete theory. Exact clock protection, common photon/tensor characteristics and a stable background have not yet been achieved together. The preceding appendices retain the historical candidate calculations rather than being retroactively interpreted as one successful action.')
h('G.1 Clock protection and the conformal obstruction')
p('Using the directly measured Yb+ E3/E2 and E3/Cs ratio slopes [20], write d ln(ν_i/ν_j)/dt=q_ij γ, with γ=7.7315×10⁻¹¹ per year. The respective marginal Gaussian 95% intervals for q are [−2.78×10⁻⁷,+1.02×10⁻⁷] and [−1.26×10⁻⁶,+4.61×10⁻⁷]. These constrain the residual differential response without importing an interpretation in terms of varying constants. They are separate summary-measurement intervals, not a joint likelihood.')
p('The earlier ε_r=1, μ_r=n² interaction preserves leading Coulomb binding while shifting leading hyperfine frequencies as n². An effective nuclear magnetic moment proportional to n⁻² cancels that leading hyperfine term and retains propagation redshift. This is a partial compensation, not a symmetry protecting all optical, magnetic, relativistic and nuclear corrections. A fixed-length cavity still shifts relative to a fixed atom. The atom–cavity oscillation study of Kennedy et al. removes overall linear drift [28], so its published oscillation bounds cannot directly constrain the leading secular drift proposed here.')
p('A more complete local matter prescription is S=∫√−q[M²R[q]/2−(∂φ)²/2−V]d⁴x+S_SM[B(φ)²q,Ψ,A]. In this conformal family, matter and light use g_m=B²q, and tensor and photon characteristics share the same null cone. The ordinary local matter action preserves its clock ratios. For ds_q²=−c₀²N²dt²+a²dΣ_k², however, material time is dτ=BNdt and the material spatial scale is A=Ba. The measured wave frequency is proportional to 1/A, so:')
e('1+z = A_o/A_e;    A = constant  ⇒  z_cosmological = 0.')
p('Choosing N=1/n and B=n with fixed auxiliary spatial geometry recovers the exponential for affine n, but the material metric becomes ds_m²=−c₀²dt²+n²dℓ². Source separations then expand relative to material rulers. The obstruction holds for this homogeneous conformal family, not for all nonmetric interactions or alternative gravity. Standard frame considerations are described in [29].')

d.add_page_break()
h('G.2 Distance and gravitational-stability consequences')
p('Deriving beam area, photon-energy reduction and arrival-rate stretching together gives D_L=(1+z)D_M and D_A=D_M for the earlier fixed-material transport construction. In a universal metric coasting interpretation of the same brightness curve, D_L=(1+z)D_M but D_A=D_M/(1+z). Both use the same present-normalized transverse distance D_M. The metric construction obeys D_L=(1+z)²D_A under photon conservation [30]. Equal brightness curves therefore do not establish equal physical interpretations.')
p('At the previous negative-curvature fit, z=1 gives D_L=5.839 Gpc in either kinematic interpretation, but D_A=2.919 Gpc for fixed-material transport and 1.460 Gpc for metric coasting. No new angular-size measurement is fitted. The fixed-coefficient flat brightness law is already known in coasting cosmology [23]; its functional form alone does not establish a new temporal mechanism.')
p('A repeated profile of the existing 960-supernova curvature fit gives R_c=4.547 Gpc and a conditional approximate 95% profile interval of 3.847–5.834 Gpc. The original four-bin flat-model residual trend has χ²=36.743 for three degrees of freedom (p=5.21×10⁻⁸ under the adopted Gaussian bin covariance). This is an exploratory diagnostic with fixed κ and calibration assumptions, not an independent detection or a catastrophic total goodness-of-fit rejection.')
p('At that radius, Einstein gravity in a homogeneous static negative-curvature background requires total ρ_energy+p=−c₀⁴/(4πGR_c²)=−4.89×10⁻¹⁰ J/m³. Ordinary positive-enthalpy matter and a canonical scalar cannot supply it. More generally, a minimally coupled scalar P(φ,X), X=−(∂φ)²/2>0, has ρ_φ+p_φ=2XP_X. Its local short-wavelength kinetic coefficient is K=P_X+2XP_XX and its squared sound speed is P_X/K [31].')
e('P_X < 0, K > 0  ⇒  c_s² < 0;    K < 0 gives a ghost.')
p('For example, the dimensionless trial P=−X+X² at X=1/4 has negative enthalpy, K=1/2 and c_s²=−1. It balances the desired sign only by introducing a gradient instability. Degenerate K=0 needs separate analysis. This restriction assumes the stated two-derivative scalar description and an applicable short-wavelength regime; derivative gravity, higher spatial gradients or other interactions can alter it. Modified-gravity examples with shared photon/tensor speeds exist in other backgrounds [32], but none is adopted here as a solution to all requirements.')

d.add_page_break()
h('G.3 Five-candidate comparison against processed observations')
p('The five retained ideas are path memory, coherent timing-field exchange, scale-dependent response, distinct matter/wave geometries and a thermal reservoir. They are not five completed mutually exclusive theories. Restricted mathematical examples can be assessed; an unspecified interaction cannot be assigned an honest likelihood score.')
p('For stationary fixed-speed path memory, d lnν/dℓ=−κ gives frequency loss but t_o=t_e+R/c gives no whole-signal dilation. Reanalyzing 1,504 DES event-average widths yields b=1.00288±0.00483 in width=(1+z)^b, with normalized-width RMSE 0.1531 for b=1 versus 0.5870 for b=0. The affine-index coherent model supplies b=1 through the ray equation. DES reference curves already incorporate dilation assumptions, so this is a derived-data diagnostic, not an independent raw-light-curve confirmation. The coherent model remains a propagation result with unresolved atomic and gravitational completion.')
p('A scale-dependent envelope proxy fits separate optical-band exponents: g 1.0212±0.0272, r 1.0043±0.0083, i 0.9876±0.0077 and z 0.9973±0.0082. A paired g–i comparison using the same 342 objects gives Δb=0.0181 and a descriptive 500-resample event-bootstrap interval [−0.0070,+0.0496]. No clear chromatic duration effect is established. This is not a spectroscopic-redshift test or a bound spanning atomic, radio and gravitational-wave frequencies; shared-reference covariance remains unavailable.')
p('The distinct-geometry idea retains a direct route beyond the conformal obstruction, but a genuinely nonconformal matter–gravity action has not been completed. The earlier negative-curvature brightness improvement is a target for such an action, not validation of it. A frequency cutoff alone cannot distinguish freely traveling and cavity light at the same frequency.')
p('For the thermal-reservoir idea, 43 FIRAS channels were fitted with equal weights of blackbodies at T exp(±δ), adjusting T and a residual Galactic-template coefficient. Diagonal χ² is 45.02 for one temperature, 45.03 for δ=0.001, 235.81 for δ=0.01 and 1,737,972 for δ=0.1. The conditional Δχ²=3.841 threshold is δ≈0.00379; this is not an exact boundary-calibrated confidence bound or a restriction on all source mixtures. It demonstrates why passive circulation and broad thermal mixing do not automatically yield the observed spectrum.')
p('A boson-mode collision law df/dt=Γ_+(1+f)−Γ_-f has a Planck stationary state when Γ_-/Γ_+=exp[hν/(k_BT_bath)]. This supplies a mathematical thermalization mechanism, but the rate ratio, temperature and energy supply are additional assumptions. Continuing redshift and stellar injection drive the system away from simple equilibrium unless their effects are balanced. Appendix H tests two such extensions without claiming a microscopic completion.')
d.save(f)
print('Appendix G added; final verification will follow the extension tests.')
